# LifeOS Console — Project ISA

> Project ISA (system of record). Lives in the project directory per PAI doctrine. Supersedes the `context/{facts,state,references,feedback}.md` foundation pattern: those files fold into the sections below and are retained as detailed appendices until fully absorbed.

## Problem

Nick's workspace holds a growing set of represented agents (one per project, stored in `~/Documents/workspace/<project>/agents/<name>/`). There is no unified interface for interacting with them: to work with an agent you open Claude Code in its project directory, switch context, and manually load its files. There is also no visibility into what agents are doing, what tasks are running, or which agent to reach for a given question. The cost is context-switching friction and no operational overview across projects.

## Vision

A local cockpit that surfaces Nick's represented agents as a roster inside a chat interface (Discord default, Slack legacy), lets him chat with any of them, runs agents via the Claude Agent SDK so they can execute real tools and tasks, and shows a live operational picture -- approval gates, task instances, delegation chains, and agent status. The agent is not a separate AI; it is a thin shell over the on-disk agent files (each agent's `claude.md` / `memory.md` / `context/`) and the PAI inference pipeline. Euphoric surprise: Nick opens Discord and his entire agent roster is there, each agent replying under its own name and avatar, running real tasks with appropriate gates.

## Out of Scope

- Remote execution or cloud hosting (local-only; Socket Mode / Discord gateway; no public URLs).
- Importing `@anthropic-ai/sdk` directly (all AI calls route through the PAI inference tool or the Claude Agent SDK).
- A separate agent store or database (the workspace agent directories are the data model).
- Native mobile app or web portal (Discord / Slack are the frontends).
- Membership or access-control integration with external club/org systems.
- Shared PAI infrastructure changes -- this project only modifies files under `~/Documents/workspace/projects/life-os-console/`.

## Principles

- Data model is the workspace: agent directories on disk, not a database, are the source of truth.
- Thin shell over real infrastructure: the cockpit orchestrates; it does not duplicate AI capability.
- Local-first: no credentials leave the laptop; no public URLs; prototype stays prototype until a deliberate decision to productionise.
- Gate before acting: destructive or outbound actions (rm -rf, git push, sudo, outbound POST, npm publish) always require explicit approval regardless of autopilot mode.
- Verify, do not assert: typecheck passes and a smoke test runs before any claim of "done".
- Keep it honest: the console is chat over personas, not autonomous AGI. Do not overclaim.

## Constraints

- **Stack is settled:** TypeScript ESM; `@slack/bolt` (Socket Mode, legacy) + `discord.js` (default); Claude Agent SDK (`@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk`); PAI `Inference.ts` for quick `/ask` path; `tsx` to run; `tsc` for typecheck; `dotenv` for config. Minimal dependencies.
- **Module boundaries are fixed:** `config`, `roster`, `inference`, `executor`, `approval`, `agentConfig`, `delegation`, `delegateTool`, `tasks`, `autopilot`, `util` (platform-agnostic engine); `discordApp.ts` (default frontend); `app.ts` / `slackIndex.ts` (Slack legacy).
- **`code/` is its own git repo** (gitignored from the parent workspace project). Commit code changes there; commit project meta/docs to the parent.
- **Discord webhook constraint:** per-agent identity (name + avatar) only works on guild channels via webhooks, not DMs. True DM-as-contact would require one bot per agent.
- **Approval gate is non-negotiable:** delegated agents run with full capability gated by their own channel's approval/autopilot state.
- **Tokens are sensitive:** `DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN` and `DISCORD_GUILD_ID` (and legacy Slack tokens) must not be committed or shared; treat any token pasted into chat as exposed and rotate it.
- Australian English, no em dashes, in all output.

## Goal

LifeOS Console reaches Phase 3 completion: persistent memory (per-channel SDK session resume and gated `memory.md` updates) and free-form `ask_user` capability (agent posts a question to the channel and resolves on the next reply), built on the verified Phase 1-3a foundation. The project is left either completed or in a documented paused state that any engineer can cold-start from `HANDOFF.md`.

